WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
A threat hunt can produce several valuable outcomes.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.705
Confirmed suspicious activity is one possibility,

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:09.040
but it is not the only measure of success and should never be promised in advance.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.700
A hunt may uncover a telemetry gap.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.480
Important systems may not be reporting, fields may be missing, retention may be too short,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.320
or timestamps may be inconsistent.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.705
Correcting those issues improves both future hunts and routine monitoring.

00:00:20.705 --> 00:00:23.120
The investigation may reveal a detection opportunity.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.360
A useful pattern can be translated into a documented analytic,

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.480
tested against historical data, reviewed for false positives,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.370
and incorporated into the monitoring program.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:35.155
Hunters may also identify asset and ownership gaps.

00:00:35.155 --> 00:00:38.250
Unknown systems, unclear business roles, outdated inventories,

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.505
and ambiguous support responsibilities can obstruct analysis and indicate broader

00:00:42.505 --> 00:00:44.360
security-governance problems.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.095
Even normal activity can provide value.

00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:50.890
Understanding expected administrative tools, service relationships, identity patterns,

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:51.795
maintenance schedules,

00:00:51.795 --> 00:00:56.175
and cloud operations helps analysts recognize meaningful deviations more accurately in the

00:00:56.175 --> 00:00:56.645
future.

00:00:56.645 --> 00:00:58.720
When evidence suggests an active security event,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.620
the outcome should include a controlled handoff to the appropriate incident process.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.275
The hunt record provides context,

00:01:04.275 --> 00:01:08.260
but response decisions belong within established organizational procedures.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.440
Hunt reporting should distinguish fact, interpretation, limitation, and recommendation.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:13.960
Leaders need a concise risk view,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.650
while analysts and engineers need enough detail to improve data, detections,

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.000
and defensive controls.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.550
Over time, organizations can measure the improvements produced by hunting: gaps resolved,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.440
detections created or tuned, coverage increased, recurring questions automated,

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.860
and lessons incorporated into future hypotheses.
